 102: ## Decisions (with rationale)
 103: 
 104: - Chosen method: one-tool-first MCP server.
 105:   - Why: repo decision and public-surface research both say one stable, reviewable MCP beats three half-built submissions.
 106: - Chosen stack: TypeScript MCP SDK.
 107:   - Why: current docs support schema-first tools, structured results, and HTTP transport; this is closer to PlayMCP endpoint validation than a local-only script.
 108: - Chosen first data method: fixture-first plus one Seoul official-data adapter.
 109:   - Why: fixture mode removes API-key dependency for RED/GREEN and smoke tests; Seoul cultural events have the most directly useful family-experience fields.
 110: - Chosen output contract: Top 3 cards plus provenance and parent-check language.
 111:   - Why: this is already shared across repo docs and minimizes report-like output.
 112: - Fallback method: if Seoul live data is blocked or too sparse, keep the same schema and run deterministic fixture mode for registration smoke while evaluating pharmacy fallback separately.
 113:   - Why: no fabricated current/live claims; the user can still demo the chat promise safely.
 114: 
 115: ## Scope IN
 116: 
 117: - Create a downstream execution plan for a new `apps/family-experience-mcp/` TypeScript MCP server.
 118: - Plan exactly one public tool: `find_family_experiences`.
 119: - Plan strict input validation:
 120:   - required: `location`, `child_age` or `child_stage`, `date_range`.
 121:   - optional: `indoor_outdoor`, `budget`, `must_have`, `max_distance_hint`.
 122: - Plan structured output:
 123:   - `query`, `results`, `warnings`, `source_summary`, `next_action`.
 124:   - each result includes title, date/time, venue/address, age-fit reason, fee/reservation/contact if present, source/retrieved_at, confidence, parent check.
 125: - Plan a source registry, deterministic fixtures, Seoul cultural-events adapter, normalizer, ranking, response renderer, and typed failures.
 126: - Plan four golden prompt checks: happy path, missing age, no confident results, and data/API failure.
 127: - Plan PlayMCP temporary-registration readiness:
 128:   - server name, identifier, description, starter messages, endpoint path, tool metadata smoke, and response visibility notes.
 129: 
 130: ## Scope OUT (Must NOT have)
 131: 
 132: - Do not build all three concepts in parallel.
 133: - Do not create real git branches until the user explicitly asks.
 134: - Do not request PlayMCP final review or one-time contest submission.
 135: - Do not claim live availability, reservation availability, child suitability, or age fit unless source/program text supports it.
 136: - Do not scrape unofficial event pages.
 137: - Do not add national/worldwide data sources before the Seoul-first golden prompts pass.
 138: - Do not store or print API keys, bearer tokens, keyed URLs, or private user data.
 139: - Do not turn the answer into a long directory/report; Top 3 only for the first MVP.
